ying to be in a profession  where i'm encouraged to take a risk  and those risks are  risk that i think enable my students to  have a better experience in school  and so for me every day  is is trying to find out how how can i  take better risk  how can i  teach in a in a way that actually  encourages others maybe to learn more  and what i've come to is the idea that  my students need to experience their  learning  i think it's mark twain that has that  quote that says a man who carries a cat  by the tail  learns something he can learn in no  other way  i bet  so i take that to mean that if i can get  my students to experience science  they'll learn things that normally they  would not be able to learn  and so that idea of creating excitement  in science  uh is something that i that it keeps my  passion high and it involves risk taking  and it allows me to have my students  kind of  fuse collaboration with others with  scientists  with trying to be relevant  and also having my students be  dynamically involved in what they do  and so i kind of bring those pieces  together and and so that's that's what  kind of stirs my passion  so  you have a couple of ways um in which  you get students really involved in you  know in science um and one of them is  you run  discovery trips  um when i was in high school it was  called coast to coast i remember  and i went on one and it was a formative  experience for me um  what gave you the idea to do these  discovery trips um and what do you feel  their purposes  that's a good question i think one of  the reasons why i have tried to take  these trips is it's like the ultimate  risk  uh to take a group of high school  students for a month  across the united states  meeting with people  and learning science in the environment  that you should learn it in so for me  it's like the ultimate risk taking  so there's a little bit of adrenaline  behind that idea  and then i was surrounded by other  teaching professionals at harrisonburg  you may remember that were very  encouraging and very supportive and so i  think those early years of support and  encouragement allowed me to take risks  that i'm still taking today  one of the things that i've noticed  about these trips  is and they are about  8 500 miles  we travel in about a month  we travel in a full-size motor coach  we take some of the seats out we put now  we put have a generator on board we have  wireless hubs so we have constant  internet access  uh the students camp and so every night  we pull up to a campsite we set up our  tents we cook our own food  but the nuggets are when we meet with  professionals and so we meet with all  kinds of people that help us understand  a variety of case studies  the last couple trips we start by  meeting with wendell berry  and wendell berry is an author a writer  and so we sit around the fire with  wendell berry and have him talk to us  about his passions for the natural world  and then we start doing case studies  whether it's agriculture  whether it's water  energy  this year this past summer we did  technology  and we stopped at google headquarters in  california  and the students were able to see the  robotics car  that drives around without anybody  manning it  and we talked about what role does  technology play in our lives the last  night of our trip we spent on an amish  farm  so we got to see this dissonance and i  think probably the dissonance piece is  the piece that i am the most excited  about if you think about dissonance  dissonance is when things come together  in butt heads  they clash  and so that's the model that i try to  use as we cross the united states  studying these variety of issues  um  you may remember studying uh the  brucellosis bison issue in yellowstone  yes i do  yes and that was a it was a big  dissonance piece uh where we talk to  a rancher who shoots the bison when they  come out of the park  we talk to an environmental group that  wants to protect the bison and then we  talk to the park biologist who's caught  in between  so we like to use that model if we can  to allow students to realize that that  within the united states there's a lot  of exciting things going on  that we need to learn about  i've noticed that a lot of our students  today cross  the ocean and go to all these different  countries but never go past the  mississippi  so part of my passion is for students to  learn their own country  and this discovery trip allows them to  do that  so when you set about um setting up i  mean now you've been doing these  discovery trips for a pretty significant  amount of time when you first set them  up how did you find  the people and the places um to  stop at how did you go about setting it  up how did you get the funding um you  know how what how did you  handle it logistically  well actually the first trip was back  before  we did a lot  with email  and so we kind of eased into these trips  without having the technology that we  have today  in fact on the first trip we took a  satellite phone  just in case we needed to make a phone  call  and i remember that we were charged over  seven dollars a minute  anytime we wanted to make a call on that  phone wow oh my goodness yeah you know  it's just it's a whole different world  but the way that i started this was  simply by listening to the news  watching the newspaper trying to find  out what are the hot issues in the  united states that we need to learn  about  and i remember reading in the usa today  about this student who graduated from  humboldt university while tree sitting  in one of the giant  trees out in california he was  protesting it being cut down  so that gave me an idea let's meet this  man let's talk to him about his passions  that of course led to the dissonance  where i said so who would want to cut it  down  well it was the rough and ready lumber  company so then we contacted them and  that's how we set up that dissonance  piece  so it's it's learning about the issues  that face us and then trying to trace  down those persons that we think can  best articulate that for our students  that's really that's a an amazing amount  of dedication um the students and i know  because i did it get a truly amazing  amount of inspiration and learning out  of these trips what do you hope that  they really gain you emphasize  dissonance a lot do you hope that they  gain  critical thinking or you know  deeper knowledge of issues  yeah that's a great question because the  the essence of this trip  is for us to understand issues from  multiple perspectives to be able to hold  those multiple perspectives in our hands  and play with them a little bit  in fact i remember one student that may  have been on your trip  that told me when we got back he said  i thought when i went on these trips i  was going to find the side of each of  the issues we studied that i would  champion  now that i'm back  i realize that my mission needs to be to  bring the parties together and have them  discuss the issues  so that's really what i want is for  students to be able to see that there  are good  points of view on both sides of issues  and for them to be able to understand  the value of listening to multiple  perspectives  and not simply jump in one camp  immediately and ignore perspectives from  another position  so that's kind of the non-scientific  values of this along with learning a  tremendous amount of science  on the other hand i mean i would say  that learning to look at several sides  of an issue is actually a very important  thing when doing science you need to be  able to give up your pet hypothesis and  say yeah i guess you know this might be  more complicated than i thought or it  might be wrong you know you need to be  able to consider  other options it could be pretty useful  for science as well  that's true and i i do think it makes  them better scientists in fact one of my  joys is to take students on these trips  that i will teach the next year  they are different students  and so it's really exciting for me to to  teach students that have been with me  for a month  and  on these kinds of explorations we  understand each other much better and we  both kind of see things  differently than we did before these  trips so you're exactly right  um so that's not the only um awesome  thing you do to help  get students interested in science you  also run a biotechnology symposium every  year  what is it and what does it do  once a year it's usually in march we set  aside a day  and we invite the local schools usually  it's anywhere from 10 to 20 different  schools that participate  they send a group of usually upper level  students  a lot of them are students currently  enrolled in advanced placement biology  we come together for a day of  concentrated biotechnology discussion  we start with a keynote speaker  we've had a scientist from monsanto  francis collins from nih has been here  wow  augusto adonai if you remember that name  from lorenzo's oil he's the father of  lorenzo he came and spoke  we've had a variety of speakers that  come and they're they're pretty  significant speakers  and then  after the keynote we oftentimes have a a  time for students to ask questions  and then we break the students up into  two groups one group goes off to and  does lab work  now we have about six or seven labs at  one time  the other half of the students will go  to additional seminars and learning  activities  and then we switch the students  so about half the day is spent with a  keynote and a major idea the other half  of the day is usually spent working with  techniques learning more about  biotechnology manipulating dna running  gels  doing a lot of the more biotechie kinds  of things  this year will be the 21st  and  usually we have about 400 students that  come  so it's been very successful and it's  very exciting and it's just a fun day  that's really neat so you know you have  all these great ideas and you know it's  clear that you really want to inspire  these students um and you get a lot of  them involved in research how do you go  about making the connections to help  students get into laboratories do some  of them work in laboratories at  universities how do you make those  connections  well that's an interesting question in  fact i had a teacher stop me in the hall  a week ago  and  that teacher said you know i want to do  what you do  so can you give me some ideas of things  i can have my students do  and i looked at this teacher and i said  have you ever heard that you need to  bend your knees before you jump  and he kind of got this  questioning look on his face and i said  you know  you need to experience things before you  can  learn how to have your students  experience things  and so one of the values i think that  teachers need to think about is  themselves getting out  learning to know people people in their  discipline so for me that would be  scientists  so that i have contacts i spent a month  up at cold spring harbor labs working on  molecular genetics and met jim watson  and talked with him on almost a daily  basis  so  that's just one example of  a place that i've become familiar with i  know scientists now all across the  united states  i make it a regular habit to wander into  the science buildings of the three  colleges that we have here in  harrisonburg  simply because i want to learn to know  them  once i learn to know them i've got a  contact  and so as soon as i have students that  have a question or that would like to  work in a lab i've got 10 or 12  different ideas immediately because i  know people  and so i'm constantly learning to know  new people  learning to know what research they do  and then working to get students  involved in those different pieces of  research so it's a constant effort on my  part to push myself  to learn to know new people new  techniques new ideas so that i have  places for my students to plug into  that's really great so um have a lot of  your students gone into you know stem  science technology engineering and math  do you have do you think you have a  higher number that have gone on to that  than average or do you produce a lot of  students that have a really good  scientific awareness in general  that's interesting question i don't have  any hard data on that one um  interestingly enough my head of school  asked me a similar question very  recently  about do you have any idea how many of  your students are medical doctors  how many of your students are  in the medical profession or are doing  bench research or somehow doing science  research and i really don't although i  do know that i have a lot  there's a lot of students that i teach  that go off and do something in in a  scientific field which is very exciting  to me  because they keep in touch  and i get to find out and now the cool  thing is just like you  now they are becoming my peers  and so now we are actually working  together where you used to be my student  now you're my peer  worker  in education and that's very exciting  for me  well i would just like to say you  haven't aged a day  you look totally the same  thank you thank you do you have any  advice for say students who realize that  they love biology and that they want to  be a biology teacher um and do you have  any advice  for  um you know biology teachers who might  have you know big ideas for ways to get  their students interested but aren't  quite sure how to implement them  um  there's a quote i think the quote comes  from eleanor roosevelt i'm not really  sure where the quote really originated  and the quote says something like small  minds discuss people  average minds discuss events but great  minds  discuss ideas  and so i think  part of my advice would be for teachers  and students alike  to try to start having conversations  about scientific ideas  get off of the i'd get off of the people  thing you know  and get off of the the major events that  have occurred in science and start  having discussions centering around  scientific ideas  and that breeds a lot of questions  and if you've got questions then you  have a directive  and so once again i'd probably  advise teachers to bend their knees a  bit  try to do things that might involve a  little bit of risk  there's an old saying that says that  people will gather around a fire like a  house fire or a barn fire to watch it  burn  and so i think excitement breeds  excitement and so when your classroom is  on fire and usually it's on fire because  of ideas  then people will gather around you  one of the one of the ways that i tell  teachers that they know if they're  successful  is they have groupies  groupies or students just want to be in  your room you know they just want to  hang around because they never know when  the next idea is going to be birthed and  they want to be present  and so that's the kind of atmosphere i  think you want to work at  is to build anticipation for when's that  next idea going to come and usually they  come when i'm talking with students  and so students want to be in those  conversations because they could be the  birth of the next big idea  um for that so for students alike i  would encourage them to start thinking  about scientific ideas learn about  things that are current and today  and ask questions because those  questions once again will give birth to  ideas for research  and and by the way don't be afraid to  ask  i'm impressed daily how you can send an  email or call someone on the phone  that is a significant scientist and they  will take the time to talk to you  and to respond to your emails  i tell my students the most they can do  is say no  so i would encourage people to not be  afraid  uh walt disney had that quote that i  like i actually have it hanging on the  wall of my office and it says the way to  get started is to quit talking and start  doing  that's that's fantastic this has been  just an amazing interview i do have one  final question though  what is the weirdest thing that you have  ever done in the name of science  probably the weirdest things involve  putting things in my mouth  um  probably licking licking the slime off  of a banana slug in redwood national  park  to show students that the slime causes  your tongue to become numb  uh is it was pretty weird  we  had a bus licking contest at one point  where we would try to see who could jump  the highest and the way you would mark  your height is by licking putting a wet  lick mark on the window of the bus  oh one time i had a contest with a  student to see who could keep a pine  needle in our in our mouth the longest  and so i kept a pine needle kind of  perched up in my inside of my cheek i  think it was for three or four days  um  but then one morning i woke up and it  was gone  so i don't know if i swallowed it or  what happened but the student won that  bit  so somehow they always centered around  eating strange things or licking strange  things  well i i will say that actually on that  trip i also licked a banana slug for  science  um and there's still a photo of me with  my num tongue having like a banana plug  yes oh that's a great moment it's a  great moment so well jumping into also  i was going to also say in jumping into  a high country lake in glacier to show  students  to demonstrate that was pretty  significant the water temperature i  think was 34 degrees  so that was that was one of those  moments i won't soon forget  so or possibly repeat  well thank you so much um for coming on  and doing this interview you have been  an inspiration to me in many many ways  and i'm sure that you have been an  inspiration to many other students  as well so thank you so much um  congratulations on your outstanding  teacher award  and uh thanks so much for telling us all  about being a science teacher  well thank you for this opportunity  bethany it's great to see you again and  i'm impressed with what you are doing  so thank you for this opportunity
 

